# The operating system a program is standing on: its arguments, its
# environment, its filesystem, its child processes, and the way it stops.
#
# The split is Go's, which is the split kanso agreed to mirror: `os` takes the
# filesystem, the environment, the arguments and the processes, and `io` keeps
# the read and write surface. Go answers most of the boundary cases and this
# module answers the rest the same way — `MkdirAll` is `os`, so `make_dir` is
# here; `os/exec` is a corner of `os` rather than a peer of it, so `run`,
# `start` and `kill` are here too.
#
# The one Go does not answer is the standard streams. In Go they are files in
# `os` and the writing is done from `fmt`, and kanso has neither files nor
# `fmt` — what it has is three verbs. They stay in `io`, because a module that
# keeps the read and write surface and holds nothing that reads or writes
# would be a name with an empty room behind it.
#
# A deliberate exit status. Exiting is a way of failing, so it is an err,
# which means it propagates by the rule that already exists and everything
# after it is skipped without a second mechanism for stopping a program.
pub type exit_status
  code

# What a finished process leaves behind.
pub type process
  status
  stderr
  stdout

# A file that is not there is an outcome a caller can anticipate, so it is
# data: `read_file` answers `text | file_not_found` and both arms dispatch
# like any values. The failure channel is kept for what the program did not
# plan for — a permission, a device, bytes that are not text. Ruled
# 2026-09-03: "if you know it's possible for them to not be there, you
# wouldn't use an exception, you'd just return a file_not_found type."
# One field, so it is a transparent nominal subtype: the value IS the path,
# carrying the tag that dispatch reads. `fn orders (file_not_found p)` binds
# `p` to the path string.
pub type file_not_found
  path

pub args = builtin_args

# An unset variable reads as none: not being configured is ordinary.
pub fn env name
  builtin_env name

# Reach it by binding, not by sequencing: `write "…" . (_ -> exit 2)` runs the
# write and then exits, where `write "…" >> exit 2` builds both descriptions
# before either runs, and the err takes the whole chain with it.
pub fn exit code
  err (exit_status code)

# A description of work answers a description, so it takes no `?` — the
# mark belongs to a function that answers true or false directly.
pub fn exists path
  builtin_exists path

# `exists` cannot say which a path is, and walking a tree needs to know: a
# name that is not a directory is a file to read rather than a place to
# descend. Answers false for a path that is not there at all.
pub fn is_dir path
  builtin_is_dir path

# Names within a directory, sorted, so output never depends on the order a
# filesystem happens to hand them back.
pub fn list_dir path
  builtin_list_dir path

pub fn make_dir path
  builtin_make_dir path

# A builtin cannot name a type declared here, so it answers `none` for a file
# that is not there and the wrapper names it — the shape `env` already uses
# for a variable that is not set.
#
# The list `run` uses would have done, and it costs the caller the string's
# type: inference gives a list index the top set, and the analyses that read
# that set stop being able to prove things about the document. jsonbench's
# decode loop stopped rewinding when the read briefly took that shape.
pub fn read_file path
  builtin_read_file path . (r -> found path r)

# The caller insisting the file is there. The bang is the choice of channel,
# and the suffix contract holds it to it: a `!` name must be able to answer a
# failure, which this one does by turning the anticipated arm back into one.
#
# It reads the builtin rather than calling `read_file` and dispatching on what
# comes back, which is the same program written one call deeper. The extra
# call costs the caller the string's type: inference reads the yield of a
# chain whose head is a builtin, and a chain whose head is a kanso function
# is the top set. jsonbench's decode loop stopped rewinding when this was
# written the other way — arena blocks 2 to 248, peak 2 MB to 260 MB.
pub fn read_file! path
  builtin_read_file path . (r -> insisted path r)

fn found path none
  file_not_found path

fn found _ text
  text

fn insisted path none
  err "cannot read {path}: no such file"

fn insisted _ text
  text

# Starting a process is a description like any other effect. The answer is
# three things, so the wrapper reads the builtin's list into a record: a
# builtin cannot name a type that is declared here.
#
# A non-zero status is an outcome, not a failure. A caller usually wants to
# read it — a missing repository answers 1 and that is the answer — so an err
# is kept for the process never starting at all.
pub fn run cmd argv
  builtin_run cmd argv . answered

# Where run waits, start hands back the process so the program decides when it
# is done — a browser told to render a page ignores its own exit budget and
# runs until something ends it.
pub fn start cmd argv
  builtin_start cmd argv

pub fn kill p
  builtin_kill p

fn answered r
  process r[1]! r[3]! r[2]!

pub fn write_file path content
  builtin_write_file path content
